From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 15:56:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] iio: qcom_smgr: use the standard soft timestamp channel

Every scan type declares its timestamp channel as unsigned with 32 real
bits in 64 storage bits. The driver pushes a 64-bit iio_get_time_ns()
value, so libiio and iio-sensor-proxy, which honour realbits, cut it to
32 bits and see it wrap every 4.3 seconds.

Use IIO_CHAN_SOFT_TIMESTAMP(), which declares it as s64, like other IIO
drivers do for their software timestamps. The scan index stays 3.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820): in_timestamp_type reads
le:s64/64>>0 for all four sensors.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 72 ++----------------------
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 66 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index cce77d0117e4..b08bbc8974c5 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -515,17 +515,7 @@ static const struct iio_chan_spec qcom_smgr_accel_iio_channels[] = {
 					    BIT(IIO_CHAN_INFO_SAMP_FREQ),
 		.ext_info = qcom_smgr_iio_ext_info
 	},
-	{
-		.type = IIO_TIMESTAMP,
-		.channel = -1,
-		.scan_index = 3,
-		.scan_type = {
-			.sign = 'u',
-			.realbits = 32,
-			.storagebits = 64,
-			.endianness = IIO_LE,
-		},
-	}
+	IIO_CHAN_SOFT_TIMESTAMP(3),
 };
 
